<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naumburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naumburg is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It became the seat of a bishopric moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> in 1028 and grew into a trading town. Its cathedral, with 13th-century statues of founders such as Uta carved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-master">Naumburg Master</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the bishopric's lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naumburg.jpg" alt="Naumburg" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RealPixelStreet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naumburger_Dom_Westseite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Naumburg lies in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, about 60 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. It was founded at the crossing of two trade routes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a> and the Regensburg Road, and lies in a wine-growing region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written record dates from 1012, when Naumburg is mentioned as the new castle of the Ekkehardinger, the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. About 1028 the family seat was moved there from Kleinjena, and in 1030 the sources call it a civitas. A privilege of Conrad II in 1033 brought the merchants of Kleinjena to the town with guaranteed free trade. Ekkehard II and Hermann founded a Benedictine monastery of St George and a house of Augustinian canons at St Moritz. When the Ekkehardine line died out by 1046, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> became the rulers of the town.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a major trading centre on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a>, and its trade fairs are first recorded in 1278. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>'s rise as a fair town from 1500 and the Thirty Years' War damaged its economy. In 1561 the Naumburg Diet recognised the unaltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>. The church lands passed to the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in the mid-16th century, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave Naumburg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> it was a centre of mechanical engineering and a garrison town of the Soviet Air Force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Naumburg has about 33,000 inhabitants. Its cathedral, a Romanesque structure with two Gothic choirs, replaced an earlier cathedral consecrated about 1042, and the new building began in 1242. In the west choir twelve life-sized statues of founders, from the workshop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-master">Naumburg Master</a>, show the Thuringian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility in dress of the mid-13th century, among them Ekkehard II and his wife Uta. The cathedral became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2018. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-nietzsche">Friedrich Nietzsche</a> spent his childhood in a house in Naumburg that is now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naumburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Otto I founded a monastery at Magdeburg in 937 and, after years of negotiation with the Pope and the Archbishop of Mainz, raised it to an archbishopric in 968, with suffragans at Merseburg, Meissen, Zeitz (later <b>Naumburg</b>), Brandenburg and Havelberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1656, Maurice received Zeitz with the secularised bishopric of <b>Naumburg</b> and lands in the Vogtland and around Neustadt an der Orla, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-zeitz">Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schönburgs were imperial ministerials from the Saale region near <b>Naumburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordships-of-schonburg">Lordships of Schönburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1028, with the approval of Pope John XIX and Emperor Conrad II, it moved to <b>Naumburg</b>, a new seat founded by the Ekkehardine margraves of Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</a></li></ul></section>
